course: everyday
lesson: 296
title: Greetings, goodbyes, please and thank you, and table manners
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Good manners are small ways of showing respect. They help other people feel welcome, listened to and treated fairly.
explain: Respect does not depend on someone's job: the school caretaker, the cleaner and the dinner lady deserve the same kind greeting as the head teacher, and shop staff the same «please» and «thank you» as your family.
explain: Greetings change from place to place. A quick «How's it going?» in Canada or the USA is a greeting, not a real question. In New Zealand, the Maori hongi, pressing noses and foreheads, means sharing breath; it is done when you are invited to.
explain: At the table, manners are about sharing. A boy in Dublin at his friend's house waits until everyone is seated and served, eats quietly, and asks if anyone would like the last potato before taking it.
fact: Greet every worker kindly, whatever their job; everyone deserves the same respect.
fact: A good handshake is firm but not hard and lasts about two or three shakes; a limp hand seems unfriendly and a crushing one hurts.
fact: The hongi is a traditional Maori greeting in New Zealand: people press noses and foreheads to share breath, done when invited.
fact: «How's it going?» said while walking past is a greeting; a quick «Good, thanks!» is the expected answer.
fact: When you introduce someone, say who they are to you, for example «This is my stepdad, Mark».
fact: When an adult says «this is my partner», a partner means the person they live with or are a couple with.
fact: «They are made for each other» means two people fit together very well and love each other.
fact: Whether two grown-ups will marry is their private business; asking can make them shy, so if a little one asks, kindly change the subject.
fact: Goodbyes vary between places and people: «See ya», «Cheers», «Take care» and «Catch you later» are all used.
fact: When a visitor leaves your home, walk them to the door and say goodbye; it shows they were welcome.
fact: Say «Please» when you ask and «Thank you» when you get something, in shops and cafés too; in parts of England people also say «Ta» for thank you.
fact: Start with «Excuse me» to politely get a stranger's attention, such as a shop assistant.
fact: Wait until everyone is seated and served before eating, unless the host says to start.
fact: Ask if anyone else would like the last piece of food before taking it.
fact: Sit with all the chair's legs on the floor; leaning back on a chair can make it tip and you fall.
fact: Eat quietly and sip soup from the side of the spoon; loud eating bothers others.
fact: At someone else's table, wait until food is offered or ask politely instead of grabbing.
fact: Never stand chopsticks upright in rice, because in some Asian cultures it looks like a funeral custom; it is fine to ask for a fork.
exercise: You pass the school caretaker in the corridor. How should you greet him? | Just as kindly as you greet the head teacher | Only nod politely if a teacher happens to be watching | Ignore him, he is busy working | Greet him only if he greets you first
exercise: Why should you greet a cleaner the same way as the head teacher? | Every worker deserves the same respect | Cleaners can give you detentions if you forget to say hello | The head teacher will never know | Cleaners are paid to be greeted
exercise: A new neighbour holds out her hand to shake. What kind of handshake is right? | Firm but not hard, for two or three shakes | Very floppy, so you seem gentle | As hard as you can, to seem strong and grown-up to her | Shaking for as long as possible
exercise: On a school trip to New Zealand, you see two people press noses and foreheads together. What is it? | A hongi, a traditional Maori greeting of shared breath | A game to see who can stay still and not blink the longest | A sign that they are arguing | A way of checking for a fever
exercise: When is it right to do a hongi? | When you are invited to | Whenever you meet anyone in New Zealand | Only with people you don't know | Never, it is not allowed for visitors
exercise: A man in Toronto says «How's it going?» as he walks past you. What is the expected answer? | A quick «Good, thanks!» | A long story about your whole day | Nothing, because it is rude to answer | «Why do you want to know?»
exercise: Your friend's mum meets your stepdad for the first time. How do you introduce him? | «This is my stepdad, Mark» | «This is Mark», and nothing more | «He lives at my house» | Say nothing and let them guess
exercise: An Australian friend says «See ya!» and a British one says «Cheers!» as they leave. What is true? | Goodbyes vary between places and people | Only «Goodbye» is correct English, the others are mistakes | They are both being rude | «Cheers» is only said with drinks
exercise: Your aunt is leaving after a visit. What is polite? | Walk her to the door and say goodbye | Wave from the sofa without looking up | Keep playing your game until she's gone | Shout goodbye from your bedroom
exercise: You order a hot chocolate in a café in Phoenix. What should you say? | «Please» when you ask and «Thank you» when you get it | Just the words «Hot chocolate», nothing more | Nothing; staff are paid to serve | «Thank you» only if it tastes good
exercise: You need to ask a shop assistant where the pens are. How do you start? | «Excuse me, ...» | «Hey you, ...» | Tap her hard on the back | Wait silently until she sees you
exercise: Dinner is on the table, but your dad is still bringing the last dish. What do you do? | Wait until everyone is seated and served | Start eating so the food doesn't go cold | Eat only the best bits first | Take your plate to the sofa
exercise: There is one slice of pizza left on the plate. What is kind? | Ask if anyone else would like it first | Grab it quickly before anyone else at the table can take it | Cut it into tiny bits for yourself | Put it in your pocket for later
exercise: Your brother slurps his soup loudly. What is the polite way to eat soup? | Eat quietly, sipping from the side of the spoon | Drink it straight from the bowl so it's quicker and quieter | Blow bubbles in it to cool it | Slurp to show it tastes good
exercise: You are very hungry at a friend's house and see a bowl of crisps. What should you do? | Wait until it is offered, or ask politely | Grab a big handful straight away before anyone else does | Take the bowl to your own seat | Eat them while nobody is looking
exercise: At a Japanese restaurant, you rest your chopsticks for a moment. Where should they go? | Laid across the bowl or on the rest, never upright in the rice | Stood straight upright in the rice so they are easy to pick up | Stuck into the table cloth | Held in your mouth until later
test: Which shows the same respect to everyone at school? | Greeting the dinner lady as warmly as the head teacher | Saying hello only to teachers | Greeting only people your age | Smiling only at the people who help you with your work in class
test: A boy squeezes a visitor's hand so hard it hurts. What would be better? | A firm but gentle grip for two or three shakes | A limp, floppy handshake | No handshake ever again | Shaking with both hands very hard and for a long time
test: A girl from Arizona is visiting a Maori community in New Zealand. How should she treat the hongi? | Respect it and share it when she is invited | Copy it with everyone she meets, even strangers in the street | Laugh because it looks funny | Refuse it because it is strange
test: A shopkeeper in Chicago says «How's it going?» as you come in. Which reply fits? | «Good, thanks! How are you?» | A ten-minute talk about your cold | No reply at all | «That's my business»
test: Your cousin with a long Ukrainian name comes to your party. How might you help friends understand who he is? | «This is my cousin, Oleksandr» | Just say his surname and walk off | Tell them to guess who he is | Say «He's someone I know»
test: Why do people walk a guest to the door when they leave? | It shows the guest was welcome | To check they took nothing | Because the door is hard to open | To lock it as fast as possible
test: Your friend says «Ta» to the bus driver in Manchester. What is it? | A way of saying thank you | A rude word for the driver | A request for a ticket | A word that means stop
test: You are at a friend's for dinner and her mum says, «Go ahead and start, it'll get cold». What is right? | Start eating, because the host said so | Wait anyway until she sits down too | Eat only after everyone has finished | Ask for a different meal
test: Why is it impolite to stand chopsticks upright in a bowl of rice? | In some Asian cultures it looks like a funeral custom | The chopsticks will break if they stand in rice too long | The rice will go cold faster | It means you want more rice
test: Which is the most polite way to get a stranger's attention? | Saying «Excuse me» | Clapping your hands near them | Whistling loudly | Pulling at their sleeve
test: dinner at grandads, my plate came first. dig in? | Wait till everyone's served, unless he says start | Yes, eat now before it gets cold, he won't mind | Yes, the first served eats first | Start with just the bread
test: one slice of pizza left, i want it 😋 | Ask if anyone else wants it first | Grab it before anyone else does | Take it, you saw it first | Split it without asking anyone
test: guy says "hows it going" walking past me, what do i say | A quick «Good, thanks!» is enough | Tell him all about your day | Say nothing, he didn't mean it | Ask him to stop and talk
test: at my mates house we had rice, i stuck my chopsticks straight up in it | Better not, in some cultures that looks like a funeral custom | Good, that's how you show the cook you've had enough to eat | Fine, that's the polite way to rest them | It means you want more food
explain: A boy in Ireland laughed and talked with his mouth full at dinner; a piece of meat went down the wrong way, and his dad had to give back blows until it came out.
explain: A girl in Ohio was rude to the dinner lady every day; when the girl dropped her tray and cried, the dinner lady helped, and the girl realised how unkind she had been.
fact: Talking or laughing with food in your mouth makes choking more likely.
fact: People remember how you treat them, and rudeness to workers hurts them even if they do not show it.
test: talking with my mouth full lol | Chew and swallow first, you could choke | It's fine if it's only soft food | It's fine if you're quick | It's fine at home
test: the cleaner said hi n i ignored him | Greet him back; everyone deserves respect | It's fine, he's only the cleaner, not a teacher | Say hi only to teachers | Ignore him, he won't mind
test: mate leaning back on his chair at dinner | Tell him to sit properly, he could fall back | It's fine as long as the floor is flat and smooth | It's fine if he holds the table | Only adults fall off chairs
test: did u hear a boy laughed with his mouth full at dinner and "choked so bad his dad had to do the thing on him". lesson? | Don't talk or laugh with food in your mouth | Only laugh at dinner if you can chew really fast first | Choking only happens with big bites | Drink water while laughing to be safe
test: my cousin says at a restaurant a lady was rude to the waiter and "got banned from the whole town". what's prob exaggerated | The town ban; but rudeness to workers does hurt them | That waiters bring food | That rudeness can hurt people's feelings | That restaurants have waiters
test: kids are saying the new boy "has no manners, he eats like a pig" cos he slurps his soup. pass it on? | No, that's unkind gossip; quietly show him if he asks | Yes, everyone should know | Yes, and copy him slurping at lunch so he gets it | Yes, and post a video of it
test: at a wedding gran says "the happy couple are just made for each other". what does it mean? | The bride and groom fit together very well and love each other | They were built in a factory to be a perfect matching pair | Their parents chose them | They are wearing matching clothes
test: when your aunt's new partner comes to tea, your little brother says "are you gonna marry auntie??" what's best? | Kindly change the subject; it's their private business and can make them shy | Ask it again louder | Ask how much the ring costs | Tell him auntie's last boyfriend was much nicer and funnier than this one
test: at the door mum's friend says "this is my partner, Sam". what does "partner" mean here? | The person she lives with or is a couple with | The person she plays tennis with | A business manager | Her brother
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the appropriate time at which to commence eating at a shared table? | When all are seated and served, unless the host invites one to begin | Immediately upon one's own plate being served, so the food stays warm | Once one's own plate has cooled | When one has finished one's drink
exam: Identify the principal reason for which chopsticks should not be left standing upright in rice. | In some Asian cultures it resembles a funeral custom | It causes the rice to turn cold | It is liable to break or bend the chopsticks | It signals that one requires a fork
exam: It is given that a stranger's attention, such as that of a shop assistant, is required. Select the opening consistent with courtesy. | «Excuse me» | «Oi, you there» | A tap upon the back without speaking | Waiting silently until noticed
exam: State the conduct expected of a host when a visitor departs. | The host shall accompany the visitor to the door and say goodbye | The host shall remain seated and wave | The host shall continue their activity in silence | The host shall close and lock the door before the visitor has even left
